- Virgulas Reference Document
  This document is the source of truth for "Virgulas", a local-first browser outliner.
  It is the normative register of features and design decisions: it describes required
  behaviour, not the implementation. When the implementation and this document disagree,
  this document wins. This document is itself valid VMD and is authored in VMD.
  + Core model
    + It is an infinite list of editable "Nodes".
      - Each "Node" has a **single line** of text
      - Can have an optional text called "Description".
        Only the first 2 lines of the "Description" are visible when not editing, showing "..." otherwise
      - Can have "Children", which in turn are other "Nodes"
      - Each "Node" text and its "Description" can have rendered markdown when not editing.
        A "Node" text is a single line and renders inline markdown:
        **Bold**, __Italic__, [links](http://example.com), ![](https://images.png), `monospace code`
        A "Description" renders inline markdown in its two-line preview, and a full markdown
        document when zoomed in: headings, paragraphs, ordered and unordered lists, blockquotes,
        fenced code blocks, thematic breaks, and tables, plus the inline elements above
        Rendered links always open in a new tab/window
        Raw HTML is sanitised against an explicit allow-list; unsupported tags are dropped
        `class`, `id`, `style`, and event handlers are never allowed; form controls are dropped
        Rendered images drop non-http(s) sources and load with `referrerpolicy="no-referrer"`,
        `loading="lazy"` and `decoding="async"`
        Rendered images never exceed the width of their container
    - Inline `#tags` and `@mentions` in rendered markdown appear as rounded pills
      Clicking/tapping a pill immediately opens Search with that token pre-filled
      A `#`/`@` token must start the line or follow whitespace, and ends at whitespace or end of line
      Tags and mentions are never detected inside inline code or fenced code blocks
  + Behaviors of a "Node"
    + FOCUS
      - When focusing a "Node" or "Description", the text becomes editable, and the raw markdown is visible
      - (MOBILE ONLY) When focusing a "Node", its "Description" field becomes visible, even when empty
      - (DESKTOP ONLY) To toggle between "Node" and "Description" we can use `Shift+Enter`. Empty "Descriptions" are invisible
      - (MOBILE ONLY) When the virtual keyboard is visible while editing, the status bar remains above the keyboard
        Implemented via `window.visualViewport` resize events that apply a dynamic bottom inset to the status toolbar
      - (DESKTOP ONLY) When nothing is focused, `↑` focuses the last node and `↓` focuses the first node.
      - Keyboard navigation scrolls the focused node into view when needed
      - (DESKTOP ONLY) `Enter` creates a new node below the focused node.
        If it already has children, the new node becomes the first child.
        If it does not have children, it creates a node at the same level.
        `Enter` on a collapsed node with children creates a sibling, not a child
      - Focusing a node in a multi-selection collapses the selection to that node
    + PASTE
      - Pasting into a "Node" text input:
        - Single-line content without a leading `-` or `+` bullet → native browser paste (inserts at caret)
        - Multi-line content, or content with a leading `-`/`+` bullet → parsed as VMD structure
          The pasted text is treated as a VMD fragment; new nodes are created as needed
      - Pasting into a "Description" textarea:
        - Always plain text paste — content is inserted at caret, never parsed as VMD
        - This applies regardless of whether the pasted text looks like bullet-formatted VMD
    + (DESKTOP ONLY) MULTI-SELECT
      - When a "Node" is focused, a multi-bullet selection can be expanded with the shortcuts `Shift+↑/↓`
        - Arrow up expands the selection to the previous "Node" of the same indentation level
        - Arrow down expands the selection to the next "Node" of the same indentation level
      - Multi-select is only possible on a single indentation level
        - Selecting a "Node" implicitly selects all its children
      - `Delete`, `Tab`, `Shift+Tab`, `Ctrl+Space`, and Move all act on the whole selection
    + (DESKTOP ONLY) MOVE
      - "Nodes" can be moved with the shortcuts `Alt+↑/↓`
      - If multi-selection is active, Move also works as expected
    + COLLAPSE
      "Nodes" can hide and show their children by collapsing and expanding
      - When collapsed, the "Node" has a visual indicator on the bullet point that it contains children, but they are not visible
        Collapsed and has children: ○ (hollow circle, rendered as SVG)
        Otherwise: ● (filled circle, rendered as SVG)
      - To toggle expand and collapse there will be a visible ▶ or ▼ button at the end of each line
        Always visible on mobile, visible on hover for desktop
      - (DESKTOP ONLY) To toggle expand and collapse we can use `Ctrl+Space`
      - (DESKTOP ONLY, MULTI-SELECT) When multi-selected, and we use the `Ctrl+Space` shortcut
        - If all "Nodes" are collapsed, then they all expand
        - Otherwise they all collapse
    + ZOOM
      When zoomed in, a "Node" and all its parents are clearly seen in the "Breadcrumb"
      - Each segment of the "Breadcrumb" is clickable and will zoom into that "Node"
      - When zoomed, the text of the "Node" is not editable
      - The description is also visible and editable
        The zoomed description is shown in full, not clamped to the two-line preview,
        and renders as a full markdown document
      - The "Children" of the zoomed "Node" will behave the exact same way as if they were root "Nodes"
      - Clicking the bullet point of a node zooms into it
      - (DESKTOP ONLY) `Alt+→` zooms into a node, `Alt+←` zooms back out
        Most browsers use `Alt+←` to go back in history, so zooms are kept as browser navigation history entries
      - The URL carries the ID of the zoomed "Node" as a hash fragment so it can be directly referenced
        If the URL contains a node ID hash on load, the app zooms into that node automatically after unlock
        This applies to all storage modes: Memory, Local, Remote, and File (including new/empty files)
        If the node no longer exists, the hash is ignored and the root is shown
      - Zooming changes the view only: it focuses nothing and starts no edit session
        With nothing focused the no-focus arrow rules apply, so `↓` moves to the first child
        and `↑` to the last; zooming never selects the first child on the user's behalf
      - A zoomed node with no children shows an empty state offering to create the first child
      - An empty root document shows an empty state offering to create the first node
    + INDENT
      - Each "Node" can be indented and unindented individually
        `Tab` and `Shift+Tab` are the shortcuts for changing indentation level
      - (MOBILE ONLY) A swipe right/left gesture can be used for indentation
        The swipe does not change focus
      - Order will be kept when indenting and unindenting. Take this sequence as an example:
        `     | INDENT B | INDENT C | UNINDENT B | INDENT B`
        `- A  | - A      | - A      | - A        | - A`
        `- B  |  - B     |  - B     | - B        |  - B`
        `- C  | - C      |  - C     |  - C       |   - C `
      - If multi-selection is active, indentation also works as expected
    + DELETE
      - "Nodes" can be deleted without confirmation when they don't have "Children"
      - A confirmation is necessary when "Children" exist. Deleting a "Node" also deletes all its "Children"
      - If multi-selection is active, delete also works as expected
      - The node is deleted if Backspace is pressed on a node that has no text
        If there is text, Backspace performs normal character deletion
        Backspace in an empty, focused "Description" returns focus to the node text
      - (DESKTOP ONLY) The shortcut `Ctrl+Backspace` is used for delete
    + (DESKTOP ONLY) COPY
      - `Ctrl+C` with a node text focused and no text selected copies that node and all its descendants as VMD to the clipboard
      - With text selected, `Ctrl+C` uses the browser's native copy
  + STORAGE MODES
    Four storage modes are available. The most appropriate one is selected automatically on each visit.
    - Memory — document lives only in JS memory; lost when the tab is closed. No passphrase, no account, no encryption.
      Default on the very first visit (no localStorage data, no remembered mode).
    - Local — document encrypted in-browser (localStorage). Passphrase created on first use. No account needed.
    - Remote — document encrypted and synced to the cloud via Supabase.
      Account email, account password, and passphrase are required.
    - File — document stored as plain VMD text in a local `.vmd` file via the File System Access API.
      No passphrase or account needed. Only available in browsers that support the API.
    + Rules for selecting a mode
      - No remembered mode and no existing data signals → Memory mode, INTRO.VMD loaded
      - Remembered mode is Memory → Memory mode directly (no lock screen), INTRO.VMD loaded
      - Remembered mode is Local
        - local data present → Local unlock screen
        - no local data → Local unlock screen (create passphrase)
      - Remembered mode is Remote
        - valid session → Remote unlock screen (passphrase only)
        - expired session → Remote unlock screen (email prefilled)
      - The remote session and the File-mode handle are only probed in the mode that is actually
        remembered: with no remembered remote mode the app neither contacts Supabase nor fetches the
        remote client bundle, so startup never depends on a network round trip
      - Remembered mode is File, valid handle → File reopened silently; if permission denied → file picker
      - Any remembered mode overrides incidental data signals (leftover ciphertext, cached email)
        Switching modes may discard local data or sign out; a confirmation is shown when that would happen.
        The Options panel action label and confirm text are mode-specific so users understand what is and is not lost.
    + MEMORY MODE
      - On the very first visit the lock screen is bypassed; the app loads INTRO.VMD from `/intro.vmd`
        INTRO.VMD is a separate VMD file served alongside index.html — not embedded in JS
        If the fetch fails, the app starts with a single empty node
      - The status toolbar shows "In memory — not saved" instead of the mode label and sync dot
      - An inline "Enable Secure Storage" prompt appears above the outline and opens the setup flow
      - The Options panel hides "Upgrade storage…" and the purge/danger action in Memory mode:
        Memory has no data to delete, and the persistent upgrade is offered by the banner instead
      - Choosing Memory mode is remembered: the lock screen has a "Skip — continue in memory" link
        at the bottom that bypasses the unlock form and loads the app in memory mode; Memory is set
        as the remembered mode, so the next visit boots straight into memory without a lock screen
        until the user selects a persistent mode. Signing out also returns to (and remembers) Memory.
      - While the app is locked, the canvas stays visible in a blurred state with INTRO.VMD as backdrop
    + LOCAL MODE
      - The first unlock creates the passphrase; later visits only ask for it
      - The Options panel offers "Lock", which returns to the unlock screen and drops the key
      - Locking flushes any pending write before the key is dropped, so the most recent edit is not lost
    + REMOTE MODE
      - The lock screen has separate Sign in / Create account tabs
      - Sign in requires the account email and password; the encryption passphrase is asked afterwards
      - The status toolbar shows the signed-in email/username when Remote mode is active
      - Signing out flushes (and pushes) any pending change while the session is still valid
        The push ignores the typing debounce, so signing out cannot drop the most recent edit
    + FILE MODE
      - The user opens or creates a `.vmd` file on disk using the browser File System Access API
      - The document is read and written as plain VMD text — no encryption, no JSON wrapper
      - The last opened file handle is remembered in IndexedDB; on next visit the app tries to reopen it silently
      - Every write is debounced: changes are flushed to disk ~1 second after the last edit
      - If the API is not supported in the current browser, the File mode button shows an error
  + ENCRYPTION
    Applies to Local and Remote modes. File mode is unencrypted (plain VMD on disk).
    The passphrase is the single secret the user must know. It is never stored or transmitted.
    - A random PBKDF2 salt is generated once per document and stored with the ciphertext
      It is not a separate localStorage key
    - The encryption key is derived from the passphrase and the salt via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256
      - Current target: 600,000 iterations, 256-bit output
      - Legacy payloads written before the envelope existed used 310,000 iterations and still decrypt
      - The derived key is never stored — it is cached in memory for the unlocked session only
    - All document data is encrypted with the derived key using AES-GCM-256 with a unique IV per write
      The IV is prepended to the ciphertext; both are base64-encoded in the stored envelope
    - Stored form: `localStorage.vmd_data_enc` holds `<salt>|<envelope>`
      Envelope: `v2:<iterations>:<base64(iv || ciphertext)>`
      Unprefixed values are legacy raw base64 and decrypt at the legacy iteration count
    - A normal save re-encrypts a legacy envelope at the current target parameters (transparent upgrade)
    - If the passphrase is forgotten, encrypted data is unrecoverable. There is no cryptographic recovery path.
      A destructive reset path may replace remote encrypted data with a new empty document and a new passphrase.
    + PASSPHRASE RULES
      - New passphrases must be at least 10 characters
      - Existing shorter passphrases still unlock; only newly chosen ones are validated
      - Changing the passphrase re-encrypts the document and pushes the new ciphertext
    + BIOMETRIC UNLOCK
      - Optional per device, offered under Options when WebAuthn is supported and a passphrase exists
      - The passphrase is sealed on-device with AES-GCM behind WebAuthn user verification
        The wrapping key is used only for this seal, never for document encryption
      - Signing out, changing storage mode, deleting local data, or "Forget this device" revoke the seal
      - It is a convenience gate, not a hardware key binding
    - PLAINTEXT METADATA
      Only the document payload (`vmd_data_enc`) is ciphertext. The following are stored in plaintext
      because they are non-secret operational metadata:
      `vmd_theme`, `vmd_last_mode`, `vmd_last_username`, `vmd_sync_ts`, `vmd_scheduled_window`,
      `vmd_inbox_node_name`, `vmd_inbox_queue`, `vmd_debug`, and `supabaseconfig`
      All localStorage access goes through the store slots in `utils.ts`
  + ACCOUNTS AND SYNC
    Creating an account enables cloud sync via Supabase. The encryption model is unchanged.
    The server stores only ciphertext and cannot decrypt user data.
    - The account password is only used for Supabase Auth — it is unrelated to the encryption passphrase
      The passphrase is set independently and never sent to any server
    - To create an account the user provides an email and password for Supabase Auth
      The PBKDF2 salt and encrypted payload are uploaded to the `outlines` table
      From this point, any device that knows the passphrase can re-derive the same key
    - Signing in on a new device fetches encrypted payload + salt from the `outlines` table
      The user enters their passphrase, the key is re-derived, and the document is decrypted
      If account auth succeeds but decryption fails, the lock screen offers a destructive reset to
      replace remote encrypted data using a new passphrase
    - The derived key lives only in memory for the duration of the session
      On page close or sign-out, the key is gone and must be re-derived on the next visit
    + SYNC BEHAVIOUR
      - Before every local write, the app checks if the remote `updated_at` is newer than `lastSyncedAt`
        If the remote is newer, the remote document is fetched and merged before the local write is pushed
        This pull-before-push strategy ensures the server always receives the most up-to-date merged state
      - A background poll runs every 60 seconds while the app is open (in Remote mode)
        If the remote is newer and there are no pending conflicts, the remote is fetched and merged silently
        The poll pauses while conflicts are pending and defers while local edits are active
      - Uploads are scheduled after typing pauses, so active typing supersedes stale background sync attempts
      - If the upload fails it is retried with exponential back-off (3 retries) then abandoned until the next write
      - Merge is performed at the node level using per-node `lastModified` timestamps and a single `lastSyncedAt` timestamp
        - Node modified only on one side since `lastSyncedAt` → that side wins, no user action needed
        - Node modified on both sides → per-field direct value comparison:
          - Same value → no conflict, either value is kept
          - Different value → conflict; the user must resolve it
          - `open` (collapse/expand) uses last-writer-wins (newer `lastModified`), never a conflict
        - Node added on one side only → kept
        - Node deleted on one side while unmodified on the other → deletion wins
        - Node deleted on one side but modified on the other → the modified side wins (deletion loses)
        - Root node is never deleted
        - After merge, any node whose parent is missing is dropped (tree integrity)
      - When conflicts exist, a blocking modal is shown; the app cannot be used until all conflicts are resolved
        Each conflict is displayed with the node text, field name, and side-by-side local / remote values
        Per-conflict: "Keep local" / "Keep remote" buttons; bulk: "Use all local" / "Use all remote"
        "Apply" is disabled until every conflict has a resolution; pressing it pushes the merged result
      - The status toolbar shows a sync indicator while the user is signed in
        Possible states: synced, syncing, unsynced (local edits not yet uploaded), error, offline
    + DATABASE SCHEMA
      - `outlines` table
        - `user_id` uuid primary key — references auth.users, cascade on delete
        - `salt` text — PBKDF2 salt, base64-encoded, generated on first use and never changed
        - `data` text — AES-GCM encrypted document, base64-encoded
          The envelope carries the IV and iteration count (see ENCRYPTION)
        - `updated_at` timestamptz, defaulted server-side, written by the client on upsert
      - Row Level Security enabled
        Policies restrict select, insert, update, and delete to `auth.uid() = user_id`
        The DELETE policy backs the Options → Delete account action
    + ACCOUNT MANAGEMENT
      - Options → Data shows the mode, the signed-in email, and (Remote) controls to change email
        and account password inline
      - Options → Delete account (Remote) deletes the encrypted document row on the server and signs
        out on this device. The account itself is kept; removing it needs the service role
      - There is no way to revoke other sessions yet
  + SEARCH
    - Search can be done with simple substring matches
    - Search is activated with `Escape` when no node is focused on desktop and with the `Search` button in the status bar on mobile
      The search controls are hidden by default
    - Search can be dismissed with `Escape` again on desktop or with an `X` clear button
    - When searching, the document becomes read-only
    - When searching, there is a live highlight of all matched words
    - When searching, all nodes that match are expanded and visible; non-matching nodes are hidden
    - (DESKTOP ONLY) Search results are cycled with `Tab` and `Shift+Tab`; `↑` and `↓` also work
    - When focusing a result by pressing `Enter` or clicking it, the app auto-zooms into the closest
      collapsed ancestor of the matched node
    - Search is "smart case": lowercase-only input is case-insensitive; any uppercase letter makes it case-sensitive
    - A result counter showing current index and total is visible while searching
  + TASKS
    Nodes can be "task nodes" with a done-state of pending (☐) or done (☑).
    The bullet point is always visible and clicking it zooms into the node as usual.
    - Task nodes render an interactive checkbox immediately after the bullet
      Clicking/tapping the checkbox toggles between pending (☐) and done (☑)
    - Task state is created and removed by editing the node text directly:
      - Type `[ ] ` at the start of a node to make it a pending task
      - Type `[x] ` at the start of a node to make a done task
      - Delete the `[ ]` or `[x] ` prefix to remove task state and return to a plain node
        While editing, the raw prefix is visible in the text input so it can be changed freely
    - (DESKTOP ONLY) `Ctrl+Enter` cycles the focused node: plain → ☐ pending → ☑ done → plain
    - VMD serialisation: pending tasks are stored as `[ ] text`, done tasks as `[x] text`
      Plain nodes carry no prefix. Task states are preserved in Local, Remote, and File modes.
    + DUE DATES
      - A task can carry a due date via the `due:yyyy-MM-dd` metadata key at the end of its text.
        The value must be a valid Gregorian calendar date
        Example: `- [ ] Submit report due:2026-08-15`
      - The due date is rendered as a highlighted chip in the node text when not editing
        While editing, the raw `due:yyyy-MM-dd` token is visible as plain text
      - The due date is shown as a badge in the task's row in the Tasks sidebar
      - Overdue tasks (due date strictly before today) are highlighted in the Tasks sidebar
        and sorted before all other pending tasks
      - If any pending task is overdue, the ☑ tasks toolbar icon is highlighted
    + RECURRENCE
      - A task can carry a recurrence interval via the `rec:<n><unit>` metadata key, where `<unit>` is
        `y` (years), `m` (months), `w` (weeks), or `d` (days), and `<n>` is an optional repeat count
        that defaults to 1 when omitted (e.g. `rec:1m`, `rec:2w`, `rec:d`)
        Example: `- [ ] Pay rent due:2026-08-28 rec:1m`
      - `rec:` only has an effect on a task that also carries a `due:` date. Without a due date it is
        stored and round-trips normally, but does nothing
      - Checking a recurring task's checkbox (mouse, tap, or `Ctrl+Enter`) does not mark it done.
        Instead its due date advances to the next occurrence and it remains pending
        For `m` and `y` units the new due date keeps the same day of month, clamped to the last valid
        day of the resulting month (e.g. Jan 31 + 1m -> Feb 28/29)
      - The recurrence interval is rendered as a badge in the node text (next to the due date chip)
        and in the task's row in the Tasks sidebar
    + TASKS SIDEBAR
      - A panel listing every task node within the current zoom, grouped into Pending, Scheduled,
        and Done sections. Tasks outside the zoomed subtree are not shown
      - Pending: tasks with no due date, or a due date that is today or in the past (including overdue)
      - Scheduled: tasks with a due date strictly in the future
        A day-window filter (3d / 7d / 30d / All, default 3d) limits how far ahead Scheduled shows,
        to hide distant recurring tasks; a hint shows how many are hidden beyond the current window
      - Each row shows the task text and a breadcrumb of its ancestors (relative to the current zoom)
        Clicking a row zooms into the nearest ancestor of the task
        Clicking the checkbox in a row toggles done/pending (or advances recurrence) without navigating
      - The Done group is collapsed by default
      - (DESKTOP ONLY) `Ctrl+Alt+K` opens and closes the sidebar
      - The ☑ icon button in the status toolbar opens and closes the sidebar on all platforms
      - On wide screens (≥ 1160 px) the panel docks alongside the document without dimming content
      - On narrow screens the panel slides up from the bottom as a sheet
  + QUICK CAPTURE
    Captures are collected into a device-local queue in `localStorage.vmd_inbox_queue`.
    The queue is intentionally unencrypted, device-local, and never synced.
    - Entry points
      - `?quick-add=<url-encoded text>` enqueues the text directly, with no interaction
      - `?quick-capture=1` opens the capture prompt
      - The web app manifest exposes a "Quick capture" shortcut, with its own inbox/download icon
        (`media/quick-capture-96x96.png` and `media/quick-capture-192x192.png`), and a GET share
        target (`title`, `text`, `url`); the page becomes the markdown link `[title](url)` in the
        node text and the shared `text` becomes the node "Description"
      - A bookmarklet offered under Options → Quick capture sends the page title, its URL and the
        current selection to those same share-target parameters
      - A payload with no title or URL falls back to using the text (or the title) as the node text
      - Captured values are trimmed; a shared value identical to the URL or the title is not repeated
        as a description
    - A capture visit MUST NOT unlock anything
      - No passphrase or biometric prompt, no PBKDF2, no decryption, no network
      - The app is not booted: no lock screen, no document is decrypted or reconciled
      - The only work performed is the queue write
      - The capture parameters are consumed and removed from the URL
      - The capture window closes itself when the browser allows it (a script-opened popup);
        otherwise a minimal confirmation is shown
    - Reconciling
      - On the next unlock of Local, Remote, or File storage, queued entries are moved in
        creation order into a configurable root-level Inbox node (default name `Inbox`),
        creating the node if needed, preserving each entry's text and description, then the
        queue is cleared
      - Memory mode leaves the queue pending until persistent storage is unlocked
      - Limits: 500 queued items, 10,000 characters per item, 100 characters for the node name
      - Renaming the Inbox node affects future captures only; it never renames an existing node
  + DATA PORTABILITY
    - Options → Data offers "Export .vmd backup" and "Import .vmd" in every unlocked mode
    - Export writes the current document as plain VMD text
    - Import replaces the document with the imported VMD and re-encrypts it on the next save
    - File mode is already plain VMD on disk and needs no export/import step
  + INTERFACE
    + STATUS TOOLBAR
      A fixed bar at the bottom of the screen containing:
      - The current storage mode, or "In memory — not saved" in Memory mode
      - A sync status indicator, visible only when the user is signed in, with synced / syncing /
        unsynced / error / offline states
      - A "Search" button on mobile
      - A "?" button opening the keyboard shortcuts modal (desktop only)
      - A ☑ button toggling the Tasks sidebar; it is highlighted while a pending task is overdue
      - An "Options" button opening the Options panel
    + OPTIONS PANEL
      - Summary: storage mode, encryption status, email/username, account password (Remote), and
        encryption passphrase are shown with inline "Change" actions
      - Blob line: encrypted size in characters, `AES-GCM-256 · PBKDF2 600k`
      - Biometric unlock section: "Enable on this device" / "Forget this device" when supported
      - Quick capture section: entry-point summary, the "Save to" bookmarklet, and the Inbox node name
      - Data section: export and import `.vmd`
      - Theme toggle
      - `Escape` closes the panel rather than toggling search
      - Mode-specific session action: Lock (Local), Change file (File)
      - Mode-specific danger action: "Sign out" (Remote, ends the remote session and clears this
        device), "Clear file session" (File), "Delete local data" (Local); hidden in Memory mode
      - Remote only: "Delete account"
      - Footer: app version and "See on GitHub" link
    + KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS MODAL
      - Opened by the "?" button in the status toolbar; desktop only (the button is hidden on mobile)
      - Lists the navigation, editing, structural, zoom, search, and task shortcuts
      - `Escape` closes the modal rather than toggling search
    + THEME AND WIDE MODE
      - Light and dark themes over shared design tokens; the default follows the OS preference
        `Ctrl+Alt+T` or Options toggles the theme, and the choice is persisted in `vmd_theme`
      - Wide mode toggles the document column between its default 800 px width and full width
        `Ctrl+Alt+W` toggles it; the toggle is per session and not persisted
    + RESPONSIVE BEHAVIOUR
      - Mobile and desktop share the same visual system with adjusted spacing and hit areas
      - Touch devices get larger hit areas for status-bar buttons, close controls, and Tasks panel pills
      - The Tasks panel docks beside the document on wide screens (≥ 1160 px) and slides up as a
        bottom sheet on narrow screens
      - The shell sizes to the dynamic viewport height (`100dvh`, with a `100vh` fallback), so the
        status toolbar stays visible above a mobile browser's URL bar
    + ACCESSIBILITY
      - Every interactive element shows a visible keyboard focus ring: a 2 px accent outline for
        controls on a surface, and an accent border plus soft halo for text fields
      - Text editing surfaces use an accent-coloured caret
      - Icon-only controls carry `aria-label`s and state (`aria-pressed` where relevant)
      - `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` collapses transitions to zero and drops the splash fade
      - Colour is never the only carrier of state where an icon or shape can accompany it
    + APP SHELL AND SPLASH
      - A full-screen splash shows the name while the app initialises, and is removed when ready
      - The document is not rendered until unlock; in locked states the canvas stays blurred
      - A capture visit that cannot close its window itself shows a minimal confirmation toast above the status bar
    + DEBUG DIAGNOSTICS
      - `localStorage.vmd_debug = '1'` enables `[debug]` console logging for internal inconsistency warnings
      - There is no in-app developer panel and no keyboard shortcut for it
  + PRIVACY AND ANALYTICS
    - The only remote script is the Umami analytics tracker, loaded with a `sha384` SRI hash and
      `crossorigin="anonymous"`
    - A strict Content-Security-Policy allow-lists only `'self'`, the analytics host, and (in
      production) the concrete Supabase origin; there is no inline script
    - Production builds are compiled without source maps and without the Supabase client test seam
    - No document content is ever sent to the analytics host; page views only
  + SITE AND DISCOVERABILITY
    - The site follows The Website Specification (https://specification.website); the attribution
      lives in the contributor docs (`README.md` and `AGENTS.md`), not in the app UI
    - Response headers GitHub Pages cannot set are applied at the Cloudflare edge by
      `scripts/cloudflare-headers.mjs` during the deploy job: HSTS, `X-Content-Type-Options:
      nosniff`, `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'`, `X-Frame-Options`,
      `Referrer-Policy`, `Permissions-Policy`, COOP, CORP, and a `Link` discovery header
      Plain HTTP is redirected to HTTPS at the same edge (Always Use HTTPS)
    - `/robots.txt` allows crawling and declares `Content-Signal: search=yes, ai-input=yes, ai-train=no`
    - `/sitemap.xml` lists the canonical URLs; `/llms.txt` gives agents a curated index
    - `/.well-known/security.txt` and `/.well-known/gpc.json` are published
    - `/privacy.html` describes what is stored, where, and why; `/404.html` is the branded
      not-found page that returns a real 404 status
    - Accessibility baseline: one `<h1>`, a `<main>` landmark, a `contentinfo` status bar, a skip
      link, announced form errors (`role="alert"` plus `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby`),
      `prefers-reduced-motion` and `forced-colors` support, and AA body-text contrast
    - While a modal owns the screen, the content behind it is `inert`
  + Design considerations
    - A splash screen is seen on load, and it is removed after the app is completely ready
    - The text of root nodes has a font-size of 1rem, the second level 0.9rem, and remaining nodes 0.85rem
      The description has a more faded colour and a smaller size (0.875rem)
    - Google font Inter is the base typography. 1rem = 14px
      Base: 'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
      Mono: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, "Liberation Mono", "Courier New", monospace;
    - All colours, sizes, and timings come from CSS custom properties; see design.md
    - Focused nodes are visually distinct from hovered ones (accent-soft background plus a 3 px
      left accent border), so the active node is always obvious
    - Light theme: paper-like warm canvas; dark theme: warm near-black surfaces; one blue accent
    - Modal dialogs share a three-part structure: header, scrollable body, footer actions
  + Technical considerations
    - The app is a static website. No server is necessary to use it
    - The document is stored in localStorage as a single JSON object, always encrypted (Local/Remote)
    - Preact and Preact Signals are the UI frameworks, bundled from npm at build time
      There is no import map and no runtime CDN dependency
    - The Supabase JS client is bundled from npm and imported dynamically, emitted as a separate
      content-hashed chunk that only Remote-mode users download
    - All crypto operations use the native Web Crypto API
    - The app shell and all static assets are cached by a Service Worker (`sw.js`) on first load
      Two versioned caches: fonts/icons (cache-first) and the app shell (stale-while-revalidate)
      Cache versions are bumped by `scripts/bump-sw-caches.mjs` only when the hashed files change
      After the first successful load the app opens fully offline (no network required)
    - Playwright E2E tests cover all the app's features and edge cases, and unit suites cover the
      pure logic (document model, parsers, crypto, sync merge, markdown, tasks)
    + CI/CD
      - The app is hosted on GitHub Pages
      - On each commit, all E2E tests are run (sharded across three jobs) plus the unit suites
      - A commit-policy job validates Conventional Commits in the range
      - On the main branch, database migrations are published to Supabase before deploy
      - On the main branch, a release job derives the semver bump from Conventional Commits, creates
        the GitHub release, and commits the version bump
      - On the main branch, the deploy job builds `dist/`, refuses to ship source maps, uploads the
        Pages artifact, optionally purges the Cloudflare cache, and attaches
        `virgulas-<version>.zip` to the release
      - Daily, a secondary workflow runs the E2E suite against the production version at `virgulas.com`
        Specs that depend on the test seam are excluded from that run
  - ROADMAP
    Ideas and improvements under consideration. Nothing here is committed — items may be adopted, modified, or dropped.
    + Feature ideas
      - Queued background sync: queue remote sync changes locally and execute them automatically once the connection is restored.
      - Load VMD document from querystring parameter. Either url of a vmd file or base64 encoded file text
      - Outline statistics: node count, word count, max depth — shown in Options or a status bar tooltip
      - Image attachments: support pasting or dragging images directly into nodes, uploading them to Supabase Storage (for Remote mode) or persisting as compressed base64 (for Local/File modes).
      - Tags (#) and Mentions (@):
        - **Autocomplete**: Typing `#` or `@` opens a dropdown suggesting existing tags/users to prevent typos.
        - **Sidebar/Dashboard**: A dedicated view showing tag frequencies and allowing complex filtering (e.g., show `#todo` AND `@alice`).
        - **Task Management**: Mentions can act as assignments, rolling up into a "My Tasks" or "Assigned to X" view.
        - **Collaboration hooks**: If multi-player sync is added, `@user` could trigger email/push notifications.
      - Multi-document workspaces: allow saving and easily switching between multiple named outlines, rather than forcing everything into a single monolithic document tree.
      - Drag-and-drop reordering: complement keyboard move with pointer-based reorder (add tests only when real drag handlers are implemented)
      - Accessibility: ARIA tree roles, screen-reader announcements for focus, collapse, and zoom changes
      - Virtual scrolling: for outlines with thousands of nodes, render only the visible portion to keep the UI responsive
      - Node linking: internal references between nodes (e.g. `[[node-id]]` syntax) with click-to-zoom navigation
      - Undo/Redo stack: keep a history of structural and text edits (e.g., capped at 100 actions) to safely revert accidental deletions or complex tree moves using `Ctrl+Z` and `Ctrl+Shift+Z`.
      - Tasks — future extensions: `@mention` assignments, a "My Tasks" rollup view across documents.
      - Sync hardening: monotonic revisions and anti-rollback, tracked in `docs/SECURITY.md`
